findings.body:
The PR adds `FromContext` to the `Logger` interface. The fake implementation `TestLogger.FromContext` (fake.go:46-48) returns `NewTestLogger()` — a brand new `TestLogger` instance, completely discarding the receiver `f`. This has a severe implication for any test that uses `TestLogger` and goes through the `LoggerMiddleware.FromContext` code path:

```go
// Test setup:
logger := &log.TestLogger{}
middleware := NewLoggerMiddleware(cfg, logger)
// ... exercise middleware ...
// Assertion:
assert.NotEmpty(t, logger.InfoLogs) // FAILS — logs went to the FRESH instance, not `logger`
```

The `InfoLogs`, `ErrorLogs`, etc. slices on the original `logger` will be EMPTY because the actual `Info` calls were made on the fresh `NewTestLogger()` returned by `FromContext`. The test will fail silently — no panic, no error, just empty assertion slices that look like the middleware didn't log anything, when in reality it did (to a discarded instance).

This is a trap for any test author who uses `TestLogger` with the new `FromContext` flow. The fake's behavior is technically correct (it returns a Logger), but pragmatically wrong (it silently breaks the test-assertion pattern that `TestLogger` is designed for). The fix is trivial: `FromContext` should return `f` (the receiver) for the test fake, since `TestLogger` does not use context for anything.

This finding is HIGH priority because it is a silent test-failure enabler: tests that would have caught real bugs will instead pass-vacuously (asserting on empty slices and concluding 'no logs' when logs actually happened on a discarded instance). The trap is set by the PR and will spring on the next test author who uses `TestLogger` with `LoggerMiddleware`.

